 },
 "labels": {
  "anya": "Шеф",
  "senya": "Сеня",
  "fedya": "Федя"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Up in German's office, Chef Barinov tries the stolen keys on the safe, and when neither one fits, he comes up with the reckless idea of throwing the whole safe out the window.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Эти ключи не подходят.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ой, я забыл второй проверить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ребята, нам надо валить отсюда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Значит так, надо сейф украсть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф, нас же посадят!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да кому он нужен?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он здесь сто лет стоит пустой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Вынести его незаметно не получится.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "К тому же он очень тяжёлый.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Давайте сбросим его в окно!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Если он сломается, ещё лучше.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Иди на кухню и смотри.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Можно сбрасывать, давай!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's walk through the dialogue as the chefs wrestle with the heavy safe.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Эти ключи не подходят.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'These keys do not fit.' When a key fits a lock, Russian uses the motion verb for 'to approach' or 'suit'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ой, я забыл второй проверить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, I forgot to check the second one.' The ordinal number 'second' acts as a noun here for the second pocket.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what are we supposed to do now?' An infinitive question with 'now' expresses urgency when a plan falls apart.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ребята, нам надо валить отсюда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Guys, we need to get out of here.' The slang infinitive literally means to tumble or pour out in a hurry — 'to bolt'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Значит так, надо сейф украсть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here is the plan, we have to steal the safe.' The two-word opener 'so like this' is how a boss announces a new plan of action.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф, нас же посадят!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, they will put us in prison!' In Russian, the third-person plural of 'to seat' is the standard colloquial way to say 'they will jail us'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да кому он нужен?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who even needs it?' A rhetorical question with the dative 'to whom' dismisses the safe as worthless.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он здесь сто лет стоит пустой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It has stood here empty for a hundred years.' Russian uses the present tense for an action that started in the past and continues right up to now.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Вынести его незаметно не получится.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Carrying it out unnoticed will not work.' The adverb 'unnoticed' sits between the infinitive and the impersonal future verb 'will not work out'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "К тому же он очень тяжёлый.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Besides, it is very heavy.' The three-word connector at the front is a useful phrase meaning 'moreover' or 'besides'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Давайте сбросим его в окно!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's drop it out the window!' Throwing something 'out' a window in Russian uses the preposition 'into' plus the accusative case.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Если он сломается, ещё лучше.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'If it breaks open, even better.' The reflexive future verb means it will break open on impact.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Иди на кухню и смотри.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Go to the kitchen and keep watch.' Two singular imperatives send Senya to act as the lookout.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Можно сбрасывать, давай!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We can drop it now, go!' The modal word for 'it is clear/possible' gives the green light to push the safe. Now listen to the whole scene again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Эти ключи не подходят.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ой, я забыл второй проверить.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А что теперь делать?",
   "gap": 0.5
